//! What a board is exposed to, and named environments to state it with.
//!
//! The ranges of the bundles below are of two kinds, and each doc comment says which. The
//! `Automotive` ones are the part operating temperature grades of AEC-Q100 (Automotive
//! Electronics Council, "Failure Mechanism Based Stress Test Qualification for Integrated
//! Circuits in Automotive Applications", AEC-Q100-REV-J, August 11, 2023, section 1.3.4,
//! Table 1 on page 3 of 44, read 2026-11-10 from
//! http://www.aecouncil.com/Documents/AEC_Q100_Rev_J_Base_Document.pdf). `Commercial` and
//! `Industrial` are conventions of manufacturers and not a standard: no document defines
//! them, and the one place that states them that was read is Renesas's page "Temperature
//! Ranges" (https://www.renesas.com/en/support/technical-resources/temperature-ranges,
//! undated, read 2026-11-10), which lists `Comm` as 0 to +70 C and `Ind` as -40 to +85 C and
//! does not say whether they are ambient or junction. A project that needs another range
//! writes its own definition of this shape.

/// The surroundings of a board, the member `.environment` of a board (ADR 0008 gave a
/// board its `.process` the same way). It states the range of temperatures the board's
/// surroundings reach (`-40degC..125degC`, or the same range in kelvin, `233.15K..398.15K`), and every fitted
/// part is checked against it: a part whose range, stated by the design or by its catalog row,
/// does not cover it is refused, and a part that states no range is kept and named in a
/// guideline finding, never passed in silence.
///
/// This interface states no number: what a board's surroundings are is the board's own
/// statement, or a named environment's (`Automotive`, `Commercial` and `Industrial` below, each
/// with the source of its range), or a project's (the manifest's `environment` names a
/// definition of this shape for every board of a project). A definition of a project's own has
/// the same field, `.temperature`, and offers its range with `?=` so that a board can replace it.
///
/// It can also require a qualification, `.qualification &= Qualification::Aec` (the enum is in
/// the prelude, ADR 0099): every fitted part whose catalog row or own statement does not say
/// `Aec` is then named, and a part that says it is not qualified (`None`) is refused. A board
/// that states none requires none.
///
/// The temperature is a range, and the checks use its two ends: the hot end must be at or
/// under a part's `.temp_max`, the cold end at or over its `.temp_min`. It is the temperature
/// of the air the board sits in. A part's own range is the figure its datasheet or its catalog
/// row prints, and the row's `notes` say which temperature that is (ambient, junction or
/// operating, as printed), which the check cannot tell: a part whose figure is a junction range
/// is judged by it as it stands (a junction range that covers the environment is a pass), and a
/// part's own heating is not added, which is the limit of the check.
interface Environment {
    /// The least and the greatest temperature of the board's surroundings.
    .temperature: Temperature
    /// The qualification every fitted part must state (`.qualification &= Qualification::Aec`).
    /// Nothing states it unless the board does: no named environment below offers it, and a
    /// board that states none asks for none and is not judged by it.
    .qualification: Qualification
}

/// Commercial: 0 to 70 degC. A CONVENTION of manufacturers, not a standard: nothing defines
/// it, and the source read is Renesas's page "Temperature Ranges"
/// (https://www.renesas.com/en/support/technical-resources/temperature-ranges, undated, read
/// 2026-11-10), where `Comm (Commercial)` is "0 to +70" and the page does not say if it is
/// ambient or junction. The range is offered with `?=`, so a board narrows it
/// (`.environment.temperature &= 0degC..50degC`).
interface Commercial {
    /// The range the board's surroundings reach.
    .temperature: Temperature
    /// The qualification every fitted part must state, which the board states if it asks for one.
    .qualification: Qualification
    .temperature ?= 0degC..70degC
}

/// Industrial: -40 to 85 degC. A CONVENTION of manufacturers, not a standard: nothing defines
/// it, and the source read is Renesas's page "Temperature Ranges"
/// (https://www.renesas.com/en/support/technical-resources/temperature-ranges, undated, read
/// 2026-11-10), where `Ind (Industrial)` is "-40 to +85" and the page does not say if it is
/// ambient or junction. It is also the range of AEC-Q100 Grade 3 (`AutomotiveGrade3`).
interface Industrial {
    /// The range the board's surroundings reach.
    .temperature: Temperature
    /// The qualification every fitted part must state, which the board states if it asks for one.
    .qualification: Qualification
    .temperature ?= -40degC..85degC
}

/// Automotive: -40 to 125 degC, the ambient operating temperature range of AEC-Q100 Grade 1
/// (AEC-Q100-REV-J, August 11, 2023, section 1.3.4, Table 1, page 3 of 44: "Grade 1: -40C to
/// +125C", "Ambient Operating Temperature Range"). Grade 1 is the owner's choice of the default
/// grade: the document marks none as the usual one, and the others are `AutomotiveGrade0`,
/// `AutomotiveGrade2` and `AutomotiveGrade3`. The grade is the range of a part's ambient
/// operating temperature and not a statement of where in a vehicle a board sits. It says
/// nothing of the other tests AEC-Q100 requires, and the ranges of AEC-Q101 (discrete
/// semiconductors) and AEC-Q200 (passives) were not read.
interface Automotive {
    /// The range the board's surroundings reach.
    .temperature: Temperature
    /// The qualification every fitted part must state, which the board states if it asks for one.
    .qualification: Qualification
    .temperature ?= -40degC..125degC
}

/// AEC-Q100 Grade 0: -40 to 150 degC (AEC-Q100-REV-J, August 11, 2023, section 1.3.4,
/// Table 1, page 3 of 44, "Ambient Operating Temperature Range").
interface AutomotiveGrade0 {
    /// The range the board's surroundings reach.
    .temperature: Temperature
    /// The qualification every fitted part must state, which the board states if it asks for one.
    .qualification: Qualification
    .temperature ?= -40degC..150degC
}

/// AEC-Q100 Grade 2: -40 to 105 degC (AEC-Q100-REV-J, August 11, 2023, section 1.3.4,
/// Table 1, page 3 of 44, "Ambient Operating Temperature Range").
interface AutomotiveGrade2 {
    /// The range the board's surroundings reach.
    .temperature: Temperature
    /// The qualification every fitted part must state, which the board states if it asks for one.
    .qualification: Qualification
    .temperature ?= -40degC..105degC
}

/// AEC-Q100 Grade 3: -40 to 85 degC (AEC-Q100-REV-J, August 11, 2023, section 1.3.4,
/// Table 1, page 3 of 44, "Ambient Operating Temperature Range"). The same range as
/// `Industrial`, which is a convention and not this standard.
interface AutomotiveGrade3 {
    /// The range the board's surroundings reach.
    .temperature: Temperature
    /// The qualification every fitted part must state, which the board states if it asks for one.
    .qualification: Qualification
    .temperature ?= -40degC..85degC
}
